  "body": "An inherited `PYTHONOPTIMIZE=1` or `2` makes the registered-package launcher print `PASS_REGISTERED_RESEARCH_PACKAGE` after skipping a verifier's false assertion. This reproduces through the complete CLI with a valid, source-pinned package fixture. With the same files and `PYTHONOPTIMIZE=0`, that fixture correctly fails.\n\nRemove only `PYTHONOPTIMIZE` from each launched command's environment. Ordinary Python verifier children and their subprocesses then retain assertion checks. Preserve other environment settings, the caller's environment, command arguments, timeouts, and explicit `-O`/`-OO` options for intentional controls. This is a tooling repair; no mathematical result, supplier certificate, frozen source, selected frontier, or kappa changes.\n\nValidation:\n\n- `python3.12 -B -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_research_*.py' -v`: all **23 tests pass** on CPython 3.12.12. They also pass with the test driver launched using `-OO`.\n- CPython **3.15.0** via the research wrapper: **21 pass, two skip** because full generic replay explicitly requires 3.12. The same result holds with inherited `PYTHONOPTIMIZE=2`. Python.org confirmed 3.15.0 as current stable; 3.12 is a compatibility exception for the pinned replay contract.\n- The new tests execute actual interpreter children, nested children, and the full registered-package CLI. False assertions reject; true assertions pass for environment values empty/0/1/2. Explicit optimization remains observable, the parent's setting is unchanged, and an unrelated environment sentinel survives.\n- Restoring only the original launcher makes all three new test groups fail (seven failing subcases). The unchanged and patched CLI probes use identical fixture bytes. A separate `compile(..., optimize=0/1/2)` oracle, independent of the launcher, confirms why assertion removal changes the outcome.\n- The selected-record check and all **25 selected-record tests pass**. No selected cold replay, hosted CI, Windows execution, or external human review is claimed.\n\nScope: this prevents accidental optimization inherited from the invoking environment. It does not prevent a manifest from explicitly requesting optimization or verifier code from changing its own subprocess environment. Those remain explicit package-author choices. Python documents the environment/flag behavior at https://docs.python.org/3/using/cmdline.html#envvar-PYTHONOPTIMIZE.\n\nBase `bce54fb3f895cbe5cd4c0eb477c3afeed100190f`; OS seed **89**, verification engineering. Fresh overlap covered 792 open PRs, 44 discussion records and 325 local records with complete remote pagination and no warnings. No pinned coordination issue exists; the scope and evidence are saved locally. PR1298 at `e76026571dbfa7299bf474b941c9abeb5bd556b3` independently fixes Windows encoding and timeout behavior. This repair leaves that behavior alone; the shared test import/insertion context needs coordination when combining patches.\n\nCredit Douglas Colkitt for the registered-package tooling and existing fixtures. Apache-2.0 headers remain intact. Prepared for Charles Bethin with substantial OpenAI Codex assistance in diagnosis, implementation, testing, and writing.\n",
